Auðhumbla:
{{wp|Audhumbla}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|non|Auðhumla|Auðumbla|Auðumla}}

===Etymology===
Generally accepted to derive from {{m|non|auðr|t=riches}} + {{m|non|[[Reconstruction:Proto-Germanic/hamalaz|*humala]]|t=hornless}}. 

The compound presents some level of semantic ambiguity. A parallel occurs in Scottish {{cog|en|[[humble#Etymology 3|humble]][[cow|-cow]]|t=hornless cow}}, and Northern Europeans have bred hornless cows since prehistoric times. As highlighted above, ''auð-'' may mean "rich"; and in turn, "rich hornless cow" remains generally accepted among scholars as a gloss of the Old Icelandic animal name. However, ''[[auðr]]'' can also mean "fate" and "desolate; desert", and so Auðhum(b)la may also have been understood as the "destroyer of the desert". This semantic ambiguity may have been intentional.

===Proper noun===
{{non-proper noun|f}}

# {{lb|non|Norse mythology}} the [[primeval]] [[cow]]

===References===
* [[Anatoly Liberman|Liberman, Anatoly]] (2016). ''In Prayer and Laughter. Essays on Medieval Scandinavian and Germanic Mythology, Literature, and Culture''. Paleograph Press. {{ISBN|9785895260272}}

