Aurvandill:
===Alternative forms===
{{alt|non|Aurvendill}}, {{alt|non|Ǫrvandill}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|non|gem-pro|*Auziwandilaz}}, cognate with {{cog|ang|Ēarendel}}, {{cog|goh|Ōrendil}} and the disputed {{cog|got|𐌰𐌿𐌶𐌰𐌽𐌳𐌹𐌻}}. The name is attested by [[Saxo Grammaticus]] as {{cog|la|Horuendillus}}. It's variously used as a [[personal name]], the name of a [[deity]] and in reference to the [[Morning Star]] ([[Venus]]).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|non|/ˈɑu̯r.wɑ̃ndilː/|a=9th century West Norse}}
* {{IPA|non|/ˈøyr.vandilː/|a=13th century Icelandic}}

===Proper noun===
{{non-proper noun|m}}

# {{lb|non|Norse mythology}} God [[Aurvandill]], associated with the [[Morning Star]] ([[Venus]])
#* {{Q|non|year=13th century|Snorri Sturluson|Skáldskaparmál||quote=Þórr fór heim til Þrúðvanga, ok stóð heinin í höfði honum. Þá kom til völva sú, er Gróa hét, kona '''''Aurvandils''''' ins frækna. Hon gól galdra sína yfir Þór, til þess er heinin losnaði. En er Þórr fann þat ok þótti þá ván, at braut myndi ná heininni, þá vildi hann launa Gró lækninguna ok gera hana fegna, sagði henni þau tíðendi, at hann hafði vaðit norðan yfir Élivága ok hafði borit í meis á baki sér '''''Aurvandil''''' norðan ór Jötunheimum, ok þat til jartegna, at ein tá hans hafði staðit ór meisinum, ok var sú frerin, svá at Þórr braut af ok kastaði upp á himin ok gerði af stjörnu þá, er heitir '''''Aurvandilstá'''''. Þórr sagði, at eigi myndi langt til, at '''''Aurvandill''''' myndi heim koma, en Gróa varð svá fegin, at hon mundi enga galdra, ok varð heinin eigi lausari ok stendr enn í höfði Þór, ok er þat boðit til varnanar at kasta hein of gólf þvert, því at þá hrærist heinin í höfði Þór."|t=Thor went home to Thrúdvangar, and the hone remained sticking in his head. Then came the wise woman who was called Gróa, wife of '''''Aurvandill''''' the Valiant: she sang her spells over Thor until the hone was loosened. But when Thor knew that, and thought that there was hope that the hone might be removed, he desired to reward Gróa for her leech-craft and make her glad, and told her these things: that he had waded from the north over Icy Stream and had borne '''''Aurvandill''''' in a basket on his back from the north out of Jötunheim. And he added for a token, that one of '''''Aurvandill's toes''''' had stuck out of the basket, and became frozen; wherefore Thor broke it off and cast it up into the heavens, and made thereof the star called '''''Aurvandill's Toe'''''. Thor said that it would not be long ere '''''Aurvandill''''' came home: but Gróa was so rejoiced that she forgot her incantations, and the hone was not loosened, and stands yet in Thor's head. Therefore it is forbidden to cast a hone across the floor, for then the hone is stirred in Thor's head.|transauthor=A. G. Brodeur|transyear=1916}}
