einir:
===Etymology===
{{unc|non|Disputed}}:
* Traditionally, and according to most sources, {{inh+|non|gem-pro|*ainijaz|nocap=1}}<ref>{{R:gem:EDPG|*ainja-|12}}</ref> or {{m|gem-pro||*jainijaz}},<ref>{{R:gem:HGE|head=*jainjaz|page=205}}</ref> perhaps further from a {{der|non|ine-pro|-}} acrostatic ''n''-stem noun {{m|ine-pro||*h₁óy-n- ~ *h₁éy-n-s}} (via collective(?) thematic derivative {{m|ine-pro||*h₁oy-n-yo-}}). Classical {{cog|la|iūniperus}} and (barely identified) {{cog|hit|𒂊𒅀𒀭|tr=e-i̯a-an|ts=ei̯an-|t=(a kind of) evergreen tree (yew?)}} are often compared, though assigning {{m+|ine-pro|-}} precursors is difficult and may rather give way to {{der|non|qfa-sub}} origin.
* Given the asynchronisms of the above, alternatively the Old Norse was borrowed from {{bor|non|la-vul|-}}, via a form {{m|la|*iēniperus|*jēniperus}} whence attested {{m|la|ziniperus}} (perhaps specifically a Western Romance form like {{m|la||*jēniberus}}, with /b/ for /p/), reinterpreted as a compound with {{m|non|ber|t=berry}} for the [[juniper berry]].<ref>This was already [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Mittelniederdeutsches_Wörterbuch/SuwtAAAAIAAJ?gbpv=1&pg=PA639 understood] by Karl Schiller and August Lübben in their 1875 Middle Low German dictionary [https://drw-www.adw.uni-heidelberg.de/drw-cgi/zeige?index=buecher&term=schiller-luebben+i&seite=g638-639&blaettere=%3C page 639].</ref> In this case, the apparent {{cog|gmw}} cognates, limited to {{cog|gml|*eyni}} (known from various derivatives) and {{cog|gmh|*eine}} (modern {{cog|de|Einbeerbaum|Einbeer-baum}}), are simply borrowings from Old Norse or one of its descendants.

