fránn:
===Etymology===
Possibly from a {{der|non|gmq-pro|*frahna-|t=gleaming, flashing}}.<ref name="Strandberg">https://uu.diva-portal.org/smash/get/diva2:1936774/FULLTEXT01.pdf</ref> Unclear etymology. The word seems akin to {{cog|non|frár|t=swift, light-footed}} only used in poetry as an epithet of serpents, and metaphor of swords and sharp weapons. Compare {{ncog|non|naðr||serpent; sword poetically}}. Also compare dialectal {{ncog|sv|från||intelligent; intelligence}} of unknown etymology (as in “gleaming/clear/sharp in the mind”). Less likely to be related to {{ncog|sv|frän||acrid; caustic; having an offensive or intense odor or flavor}}, {{ncog|no|fræna||to give off a strong odor}}.

The root has been found in two Scandinavian placenames. 
* {{cog|gmq-oda|Franninge}}, {{m|gmq-oda|Fræningæ}}, {{m|gmq-oda|Fræninge}}, {{m|gmq-oda|Frenninge}}, {{cog|sv|Fränninge}}, a Scanian locality. The locality is assumed to have been named after the inhabitants, {{cog|gmq-oda|*fræningar}}, called so after the local river (today Vollsjöån), {{cog|gmq-oda|*Frān|g2=f}} (< {{cog|gmq-pro|*Frahnō}}), or {{m|gmq-oda|*Frāna|g2=f}} (< {{m|gmq-pro|*Frahnōn-}}), formed from an Old Danish cognate adjective to ''fránn'' (“the shimmering river”).<ref name="isof">https://www.isof.se/namn/namnbloggen/inlagg/2019-09-03-franninge</ref><ref name="Strandberg"/>
* {{cog|gmq-osw|Franøø}}, {{desc|sv|Frånö|t=gleaming island}}.<ref>https://www.isof.se/namn/ortnamn/sol/ortnamnslexikon/fagered-forslov</ref>

Compare the potential cognate lineages:
* {{cog|gmq-osw|frar}}, {{m|gmq-osw|fra}}, {{cog|sv|frår}}, {{m|sv|frå|t=dawned, awake}}; {{cog|osx|fraho}}, {{m|osx|frō}}, {{cog|goh|frao}}, {{m|goh|frō}}, {{cog|de|froh}}; from a root meaning “brisk”.
* {{cog|gmq-osw|fror}}, {{m|gmq-osw|fro}}, {{cog|sv|fro|t=vigorous, brisk, well; merry}}; {{cog|gml|vrō}}, {{cog|de|froh|t=merry}}
* {{cog|gmq-osw|frana}}, {{cog|sv|fråna|t=to be eager, to become eager; to be revived, to be refreshed; to come around, perk}}

===Adjective===
{{non-adj}}

# {{lb|non|poetic|especially of swords and serpents}} [[gleaming]], [[flashing]]
#* {{quote-book|non|year=10th century|author=Anonymous|title=Vǫluspá|text=Þar kømr inn dimmi · dreki fljúgandi,<br>naðr '''fránn''', neðan · frá Niðafjǫllum.|t=There comes the dim dragon flying, a '''gleaming''' adder, below from the Niðafjöll.}}
