gandr:
===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|non|gandʀ}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|non|/ˈɡandr̩/}}

===Etymology 1===
From an earlier {{m|non|gandʀ}} (pre 11th c.), from {{inh|non|gmq-pro|*ᚷᚨᚾᛞᚨᛉ}}, as attested in adjective {{m|gmq-pro|ᚢᚾᚷᚨᚾᛞᛁᛉ||not bewitched}}, from {{inh|non|gem-pro|*gandaz}}. Root sense, “magic”? or “spirit”?, is unknown.

Unclear relations to [[#Etymology 2|Etymology 2]].

One idea could be a derivation from {{der|non|gem-pro|*gāndz}} ({{cog|non|gándi}}, {{m|non|gáandi}}, “going”), present participle of {{m|gem-pro|*gāną}} ({{cog|non|gá}}, “to go”), i.e. with {{m|gem-pro|*-az|pos=agent suffix}}, thus meaning “walker”, which would fit both Etymology 1: “magic spell” {{gl|traveling magic}} and “familiar” {{gl|following spirit}}, as well as Etymology 2: “walking staff”. Compare partially related (Etymology 2) {{cog|sv|gangel}}, {{m|sv|gångel|t=branch, stock, stilt, etc}}, {{cog|de|Gängel|t=cradle runner}}, to the same root of “going” (cf. {{cog|de|gängeln|t=to make walk}}).

====Noun====
{{head|non|noun|g=m|genitive|gands}}

# {{senseid|non|magic}} that of [[black magic]], [[witchery]]; [[bewitchment]]; ''further'': that (potentially) caused by witchery
#:{{hyponyms|non|gandferð<t:witchcraft-travel>}}
## [[nausea]], [[heartache]] {{gloss|potentially caused by witchery}}
## [[tempest]], [[storm]], that of wind {{gloss|potentially caused by witchery}}
## {{lb|non|figuratively}} that of [[harm]] {{gloss|potentially caused by witchery}}
##:{{ux|non|hallar '''gandr'''|building '''damage''' (fire)}}
##:{{ux|non|selju '''gandr'''|willow '''damage''' (wind)}}
# {{senseid|non|spirit}} “gand”; foul spirit, a witch’s [[familiar]]
#* ''{{w|Fóstbrœðra saga|Fóstbrǿðra saga}}'' (“The Saga of the Sworn Brothers”), chapter 23
#*: {{quote|non|En er hún vaknar hratt hún mǿðilega ǫndunni. Bǫðvarr son hennar mælti þá: “Þú lézt illa í svefni móðir, eða hefir nokkut fyrir þik borit?" Þórdís svarar: "Víða hefi ek '''gǫndum''' rennt í nótt; ok em ek nú vís orðin þeira hluta, er ek vissa eigi áðr."|And, awakening, she was painfully short of breath. Badwar, her son, then said: "You sounded pained in your sleep, mother, or has something happened to thee?" Thurdis answers: "I have roamed far with my '''gands''' tonight, and am now wise about such things as I knew not before."}}
#* ''{{w|Þiðreks saga}}'', chapter 394 (352)
#*: {{quote|non|Her eptir samnaz saman með Hertnið konungi mikill her. Ok hans kona Ostacia ferr út ok rærði sinn '''gand'''—þat kǫllom vér at hon færi at seiða, svá sem gǫrt var í forneskju—at fjǫlkungar konur, þær er vér kǫllom vǫlur, skyldu seiða hónum seið. Svá mikit gerði hon af sér i fjǫlkyngi ok trollskap, at hon seiddi til sin margskonar dýr, léona ok bjǫrnu ok flugdreka stóra. Hon tamði þá alla þar til at þeir lýddo henni, ok hon mátti vísa þeim á hendr sínom úvinum.|After this a great army gathers around king Hertnid, and his wife Ostacia goes out and reared her '''gand'''—by which we mean that she went out to practice magic ({{m|non|seiðr}}) as was done in ancient times—that the sorcerous women which we call {{m|non|vǫlva|walas}} should aid him by seiðr. She accomplished so much by sorcery and trollship that she bewitched for herself all kinds of animals; lions and bears and great flying dragons. She tamed them all until they obeyed her and she could set them upon her enemies.}}
## objects which a witch rides on: [[serpent]], [[wolf]], [[horse]]
##:{{hyponyms|non|Vánagandr<t:wolf of the river Ván: Fenrir>}}
## [[wolf]], [[outlaw]]
##:{{syn|non|vargr}}

=====Usage notes=====
This word, though commonly glossed simply as “staff” or “wand” (see [[#Etymology 2|Etymology 2]]), is by no means a neutral term. In ''{{w|Historia Norwegiae}}'' (1200), written in Latin, it is exclusively used in the context of a peculiar type of sorcery. Here follows translations by Peter Fischer (2003) of the relevant sections; for the original Latin text, see the entry for {{cog|la|gandus}}:

* There are some who are worshipped by the ignorant masses as though they were prophets, since, whenever questioned, they will give many predictions to many folk through the medium of a foul spirit which they call “'''gand'''”, and these auguries come true. Furthermore they attract to themselves desirable objects from distant parts in an astounding fashion and miraculously reveal hidden treasures, even though they are situated a vast distance away.
* Next they consulted another specialist in the magic arts as to what had happened in each case. This individual went through all his practices in similar fashion, though with a different outcome: the hostess arose in sound health and then he revealed to them that the sorcerer had died in the following way: his '''gand''', having taken on the likeness of a whale, was shooting rapidly through a lake when it had the misfortune to encounter a hostile '''gand''', which had transformed itself into sharply pointed stakes; these stakes, hidden in the depths of the lake, penetrated the repulsed creature's belly, and this was also manifested by the death of the magician in the house.

Its descendants includes the objects which a witch rides on, including serpents, wolves and horses, and their magic staffs, potentially related to the later myth of witches riding brooms. Compare also “gand-fly” ({{m+|non|gandfluga}}, {{cog|is|ganfluga}}, {{cog|nn|gandfluge}}, {{cog|sv|ganfluga}}), “magic force in form of a fly”, usually a disease sent through air by a wizard. Also as {{ncog|non|galdrafluga|lit=galder-fly}}.

