gripr:
===Etymology 1===
[[File:Södermanlands vapen.svg|thumb|Swedish COA:s features griffins already in the 13th century]]

Likely via {{der|non|nds|-}}, from {{der|non|la|gryphus}}, ultimately from {{der|non|grc|γρύψ|t=griffin}}. It's unclear when it enters the Norse language; appearing as ''gripr'' in {{w|Þiðreks saga}} (circa 1250, Norway), later as {{cog|gmq-osw|griper}} (1346) and {{cog|gmq-oda|gryp}}. Danish-Norwegian descendants carries the sense of “vulture” ({{cog|da|grib}}, {{m|da|gribb}}; {{cog|no|gribb}}), while the Swedish descendant carries the sense of “griffin” ({{cog|sv|grip}}).

This spelling coincides with {{der|non|gml|grīp}} ({{cog|nds|Grip}}; also {{cog|br|gripi}}); via the fairy tale {{w|The Griffin (fairy tale)|The Griffin}}, also found in {{cog|nl|grijp}}, {{m|nl|grijpvogel}}, {{cog|no|fugl grip}}, and {{cog|is|gripfugl}}. Compare with the High German spelling: {{cog|goh|grif}}, {{cog|de|Greif|t=griffin}}; and Latin spelling: {{cog|la-med|grȳphō}}; {{cog|gmq-oda|gryph}}, {{cog|da|grif}}; {{cog|no|griff|t=griffin}}.

====Noun====
{{non-noun|m|}}

# {{lb|non|mythology}} ''based on descendants'': [[griffin]]
# {{lb|non|{{w|Þiðreks saga}}}} [[vulture]]<ref name="Cleasby"/>
#: {{syn|non|gammr<q:based on descendants>}}

=====Alternative forms=====
