Guðrún:
===Etymology===
Probably a mix of two originally distinct names, both with the latter part {{m|non|rún||rune, secret, confidante}}: {{m|non|Guðrún}}, {{m|non|Goðrún}}, from {{m|non|guð||god}} ({{der|non|gem-pro|*Gudarūnō}}) and (perhaps more common) {{m|non||*Gunnrún}}, {{m|non|Guðrún}}, from {{m|non|gunnr}}, {{m|non|guðr||battle}} ({{der|non|gem-pro|*Gunþarūnō}}), with the regular sound change {{m|non||-nnr-}} > {{m|non||-ðr-}}. The latter is evidenced by the Icelandic short form {{m|is|Gunna}}.

A heroine of several Norse legends, identical to [[Kriemhild]] in the German ''Nibelungenlied''.

===Proper noun===
{{non-proper noun|f}}

# {{given name|non|female}}

====Descendants====
* {{desc|da|Gudrun}}
* {{desc|fo|Guðrun}}
* {{desc|is|Guðrún}}
* {{desc|nn|Gudrun|Guro}}
* {{desc|sv|Gudrun}}

The name of the Norwegian folklore character ''Guro Rysserova'' is derived from the Old Norse heroine, and the part ''Rysserova'' means probably a horse’s tail, being not related to Swedish {{m|sv|ryssrova||Turkish wartycabbage}}. The legendary Gudrun is also called ''Jorunn Joklekåpa'' in Norwegian ballades derived from the [[w:Völsunga saga|Völsunga saga]]. According to [[w:Moltke Moe|Moltke Moe]] and [[w:Sophus Bugge|Sophus Bugge]], this name consists of a female name {{m|nn|Jorunn}}, which replaced Gudrun because of similar sound, and a mix of the names of {{m+|non|Gjúki}} and {{m|non|Buðli}} with ending {{m|nn|kåpe}}.

===Further reading===
* [[w:nn:Eivind Vågslid|Eivind Vågslid]], ''Norderlendske fyrenamn'', 1988, {{ISBN|82-991668-0-2}}
* Roland Otterbjörk, ''Svenska förnamn'', Stockholm 1964

