Gungnir:
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
{{surf|non|*gunga|-na|-ir}} (“the swaying one, the rocker”). Compare {{m|non|Mjǫllnir|t=the miller → the crusher}}.

The root verb is attested way later as {{cog|da|gynge}} (older {{m|da|gunge}}), {{cog|sv|gunga|t=to swing, to sway}}, ablaut of {{m|non|ganga|t=to go}} (cf. {{m|non|*gænga|t=lane, path}}), to the same root as {{cog|en|going}}, among many more.

The name means that the spear/lance rocks in balance with the horse and thus don't miss.

Since the root sense is attested late, a potential alternative reading could be more direct sense of {{m|non|ganga|t=to go}}, thus maybe “charger, launcher”? {{gloss|i.e. the lance/the javelin}}

===Proper noun===
{{head|non|proper noun}}

# {{lb|non|Norse mythology|and|paganism}} The spear/lance of the Norse god [[Odin]], which is believed to never miss its target.

====Descendants====
