helvíti:
===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|non|hælvīti||oen}}

===Etymology===
Comprising of {{af|non|hel|t1=the death, underworld|víti|t2=penalty, punishment|lit=penalty/punishment in death}}, thought to stem from a Christian Pan-Germanic construction as {{inh|non|gem-pro|*haljōwītiją}},<ref>{{R:non:AnEW|page=222|head=helviti}}</ref> then likely a calque from a {{der|non|gmw|-}} language, or an earlier {{der|non|gmw-pro|*halljawītī}}, since the Norse were the last major Germanic branch to get Christianized (the first Norse Christians appear in the late 8th century, give or take). The term appears prior to Old Norse in {{cog|ang|hellewite}}, {{cog|osx|helliwiti|helliwīti}}, {{cog|goh|helliwizi|helliwīzī}}, {{m|goh|hellawizi|hellawīzī}}.

The fore element, {{m|non|hel}}, means both “the death” and “underworld”, and is also used for the Norse personification of death, the goddess {{w|Hel (mythological being)|Hel}}. Compare {{cog|non-own|í hel}}, {{cog|non-oen,gmq-osw,gmq-oda|i hæl}}, {{cog|gmq-osw-lat|ihiæl}}, {{cog|sv|ihjäl}}, Late {{cog|gmq-oda|i hiel}}, {{cog|da|ihjel||to the death, to the underworld}}. It stems from {{der|non|gem-pro|*haljō}}, ultimately from {{der|non|ine-pro|*ḱel-||to cover, hide, conceal}}, which is used here in regards to burrying the dead and believing them to reside underground in the “underworld”.

===Noun===
{{non-noun|n}}

# [[hell]], [[underworld]] [[punishment]]

