kreppa:
===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|non-oen|krympa||oen}}
* {{alt|non-oen|*kræppa||oen}}
** {{desc|sv|kräppa}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|non|gem-pro|*krampijaną}}, causative of {{m|gem-pro|*krimpaną}}.

The Old West Norse form is ''kreppa'', following an Old Norse development in the 7th century with the assimilation of ''-nt-'', ''-nk''-, ''-mp-'' into ''-tt-'', ''-kk-'', ''-pp-''. The East Norse form descends from {{m|non|*krimpa}} the strong verb, and, {{m|non|*krympa}}, the zero-grade form remained partially more archaic, giving the form {{m|non-oen|krimpa}}.<ref>Sandøy, H. (2005). XVII [https://www.academia.edu/77092024/202_The_typological_development_of_the_Nordic_languages_I_Phonology §202. The typological development of the Nordic languages I: Phonology. pp. 1852–1872]</ref> An analog East Norse form is however found in dialectal {{cog|sv|kräppa}}, indicating an {{cog|non-oen|*kræppa}}.

Compare {{m|non-own|spretta}}, {{m|non-oen|*sprætta}}, causative of {{m|non-own|*sprenta}}, {{m|non-oen|*sprinta}}.

===Noun===
{{non-noun|f|kreppu}} — ''[[Old West Norse]]''

# [[strait]], [[scrape]]
#: {{syn|non|krepping}}

