Loki:
===Etymology===
Now generally seen as deriving from {{cog|non|lok||lock}}, equivalent to {{suf|non|lok|-i}}, from {{inh|non|gem-pro|*luką||lock}}, from {{der|non|ine-pro|*lewg-||to turn, bend}}, connecting his name to entanglement. ''Loki'' may have originated as an epithet or title ("Entangler"), similar to ''[[Freyr]]'' ("Lord") for ''[[Yngvi]]'', as evidenced by the [[Jötunn]] [[Útgarða-Loki]] featuring the same element.

Some have alternatively suggested Loki's original name was {{cog|non|*logi||liar, deceiver}} (from {{der|non|ine-pro|*lewgʰ-||to lie, tell a lie}}, cognate with {{cog|ang|loga||liar, deceiver}}), as he is the god of mischief, or {{cog|non|logi||fire, blaze}}, if he was viewed as being connected with fire.<ref>{{cite-journal|author=Heide, Eldar |url=http://eldar-heide.net/Publikasjonar%20til%20heimesida/Loke-artikkel%20til%20nettsida.pdf |title=Loki, the ''Vätte'', and the Ash Lad: A Study Combining Old Scandinavian and Late Material|journal=Viking and Medieval Scandinavia|volume= 7 |year=2011|pages=63–106 (65–75, quoting p. 75)|doi=10.1484/J.VMS.1.102616|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260602154408/https://eldar-heide.net/Publikasjonar%20til%20heimesida/Loke-artikkel%20til%20nettsida.pdf|archivedate=2 June 2026}}</ref> In popular folk etymology, his name often assumed to be a variant of {{cog|non|logi||flame, blaze}} (from {{der|non|gem-pro|*lugô||flame, blaze}}; compare {{cog|non|leygr||flame, blaze}}, from {{der|non|gem-pro|*laugiz||flame, blaze}}, from {{der|non|ine-pro|*leuk-||light; white; to shine}}), but this is not linguistically sound.

