sax:
===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|non|saks}}

===Etymology===
{{root|non|ine-pro|*sek-|id=cut}}
From {{inh|non|gem-pro|*sahsą||dagger, knife}}. Ultimately from {{der|non|ine-pro|*sek-||to cut}}.

Compare Early Modern Swedish {{m|sv|enbak||sax knife|lit=single-back}}.

The single-beveled design appears to be the reason the term was applied to shears and scissors. In Sweden, a technical term for 'single-beveled edge profile' is “sax profile” ({{m|sv|saxslipning}}; common for {{w|broadaxe|broadaxes}}), which may be an Old Norse technical term originally.

===Noun===
{{non-noun|n|sax|sǫx}}

# [[sax]], a single-[[beveled]] one-edged large work knife, a [[machete]] {{gl|broadly}}
## shorter [[backsword]] {{gl|really a weaponized sax; compare {{cog|ang|scramasax||combat sax}}}}
# {{lb|non|in the plural}} [[shears]], [[scissors]]

====Use====
Saxes were predominantly single-beveled for ease of sharpening (a common machete type trait), and were used as large work knifes akin to the modern machete. This class of large knife has been common around the world historically. Such were used for cutting away vegitation, self-defence, and other everyday duties which could not be performed by a regular-size knife. Swords, even if similar, are not used for knife-duties.

