skrælingi:
{{was fwotd|non|2018|January|11}}

===Etymology===
{{unc|non}}. There are several suggested origins: 
* Derived from an unattested adjective {{m|non|*skræll|t=poor, puny}}, compare {{cog|no|skral|t=poor, ill, bad}}, {{cog|nl|schraal|t=poor, scanty}};<ref name=IO>{{R:is:IO|skrælingi}}</ref>
* Fortescue et al. (1994) propose relation to {{m|non|skrá|t=dried skin}}, in reference to the animal pelts worn by the Inuit;<ref name=IO /><ref>{{cite-book|en|author=Michael Fortescue;Steven Jacobson;Lawrence Kaplan|year=1994|title=Comparative Eskimo dictionary: with Aleut cognates|entry=kalaaliq|location=Fairbanks|publisher=Alaska Native Language Center|isbn=1-55500-051-7|page=153|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/fortescue-jacobson-kaplan-comparative-eskimo-dictionary-1994/page/153/mode/1up?q=skr%C3%A6lingi&view=theater|column=2}}</ref>
* May be linked to {{m|non|skrælna||to be shrivelled, e.g. by the sun}};<ref name=IO /> 
* {{onomatopoeic|non}} origin has been proposed by William Thalbitzer (1932), compare {{m|non|skrækja|t=to screech, shriek}}.<ref>{{cite-book|da|author=William Thalbitzer|year=1932|title=Fra Grønlandsforskningens første dage|location=Copenhagen|publisher=Bianco Luno|page=14}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|non|/ˈskrɛːlɪ̃ŋɡɪ/|a=12th century Icelandic}}

===Noun===
{{non-noun|m|skrælinga|skrælingar|pl2=skrælingjar}}

# a native of [[Greenland]] or [[Vinland]]
#* {{quote-book|non|title=w:Eiríks saga rauða|trans-title=Saga of Erik the Red|year=a. 1265|chapter=12|title2=w:Hauksbók|compiler2=w:Haukr Erlendsson|year2=a. 1334|url2=https://handrit.is/manuscript/view/da/AM04-0544|footer=translation by J. Sephton, 1880
|text=Þat bar til ad gridungr liop or skoge er þeir karlsefni attu ok gellr hat. þat felast vid '''skrelingar''' ok laupa vt a keipana ok ʀeru sidan sudr fyri landit. verdr þa ekki uart uid þa þríar uikur i samt.
|norm=Þat bar til, at griðungr hljóp ór skógi, er þeir Karlsefni áttu, ok gellr hátt. Þetta fælast '''skrælingar''' ok hlaupa út á keipana ok reru síðan suðr fyrir landit. Verðr þá ekki vart við þá þrjár vikur í samt.
|t=Now it came to pass that a bull, which belonged to Karlsefni's people, rushed out of the wood and bellowed loudly at the same time. The '''Skrælingar''', frightened thereat, rushed away to their canoes, and rowed south along the coast. There was then nothing seen of them for three weeks together.}}

