===Etymology===
Compound of {{com|non|vika|t1=sequence, shift|sjór|alt2=sjóvar|t2=sea|pos2=genitive}}, “sea sequence, sea shift” – compare synonym {{ncog|no|roskift|lit=rowing shift}}. Cognate to {{cog|gml|weke sees|wēke (sees)|pos=genitive}}; maybe to an earlier {{inh|non|gem-pro|*wikǭ saiwīz|*wikǭ (saiwīz)}}.

The term is really the fore-element {{m|non|vika}}, an old Germanic word relating to movement (among much more); {{inh|non|gem-pro|*wikǭ}}, from {{der|non|ine-pro|*weyg-||to bend, wind, turn, yield}}. Compare {{m|non|víkja||to move, to change position}}.

By relation probably the root of {{m|en|víkingr}}.

