{{ety|non|id=eider}}
{{unc|non}}. Cognate to {{cog|smi-pro|*(h)āvtë}}, either from a common unattested language or through mutual loans. {{cog|sa|आति|t=a type of aquatic bird}} has been suggested, but it is inconsistent with either Sami cognates or the unattested masculine form {{l|non|Reconstruction:Old Norse/áðr|*áðr}}, both suggesting a Proto-Germanic ''*aw(V)diz'', probably {{der|non|gem-pro|*awadiz}}. Derivations from an ultimate Proto-Indo-European {{m|ine-pro|*h₂éwis}} are considered "unconvincing" by Guus Kroonen, which strengthens the non-Indo-European {{der|non|qfa-sub|-}} hypothesis.

According to Watkins, from a {{der|non|gmq|-}} root {{m|gmq-pro|tr=*athi-}} (sc. ''*āði-''), from {{cog|gem-pro|*ethi-}} (sc. ''*ēþi-''), from a theoretical {{der|non|ine-pro|-}} root {{m|ine-pro||*eti-|t=eider}} (sc. ''*ḗti-''?).<ref>{{R:Online_Etymology_Dictionary|eider}}</ref>

Compare the difficulty in precising the relationship between Old Norse {{l|non|igða|t=small bird}} and {{m+|sia|avigʒinĉ|t=chickadee}}.

