úr:
===Etymology===
Potentially from {{inh|non|gem-pro|*ūrą|t=water, rain; wetness}}, from {{der|non|ine-pro|*uh₁r-}}, zero grade form of {{m|ine-pro|*weh₁r-|t=water}}. Related to {{cog|ang|ūrig|t=moist}}, {{cog|la|ūrīna|t=urine}}. Also {{cog|non|*var|*vār|t=pus, bodily gunk}}, {{cog|ang|wær|t=sea}}, {{cog|prg|wurs|t=pool}}, {{cog|sa|वार्|tr=vā́r|t=water}}.

The many descendants show a clear connection with precipitation, often coupled with wind, but varies greatly in terms of what precipitation is specified, from snow, to rain, to drizzle etc, as well as the strength of wind, from light wind, to storm. Compare dialectal {{cog|da|ur||haze}}, Old {{cog|is|úr||drizzle, spray}}, {{cog|no|ur||rainy weather in the mountains}}, {{m|no|yr||drizzle; dew; snowfall}}, {{m|no|yra||to drizzle}}, {{cog|sv|ur||windy precipitation; blustery and profuse snowfall}}, {{m|sv|i ur och skur||through stormy snow and rain|lit=in ''ur'' and shower}}, {{m|sv|yr||windy precipitation; blustery and profuse snowfall; swirling, bluster; drizzle, spray}}, {{m|sv|yra||snowstorm}}, dialectal {{cog|sv|ura||to drizzle}}, {{cog|gmq-gut|starur||springtime snowy or rainy weather|lit=starling-ur}}.

The common wind/storm-component might be a later introduction; compare the compunds of {{cog|no|yrvær||blowing snow, snowstorm}}, {{cog|sv|urväder||bad weather involving ''ur''}}, {{m|sv|yrväder||blustery weather; blowing snow, snowstorm}}, recorded from ca. 1430, compounding ''ur/yr'', with ''weather'', which initially meant “air in motion, blowing”. However, if related to {{cog|gsw|urig|t=ragin (of the weather)}}, then the wind/storm component could be an older Pan-Germanic sense. Further compare potential further related words, like dialectal {{cog|sv|urrig|t=ruffled}}, {{m|sv|urra|t=to ruffle}}, {{m|sv|urr|t=ruffle}}, which all also have analog senses regarding shivering.

